WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.640
Let's talk about religion this week.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.300
Whether you're a believer or a non-believer,
religion has, and probably always will, play

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.050
some role in society.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:17.040
This is why religion has been at least a part
of so many of the discussions I've had on

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.090
this show, from interviews with an atheist
like Sam Harris, to a Catholic like Bishop

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:27.790
Barron, to a christian Dinesh D'Souza, to
orthodox Jew like Ben Shapiro, to ex-muslim

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:33.891
Yasmine Mohammed to atheist Muslim Ali Rizvi,
to Jew turned atheist turned Christian like

00:00:33.891 --> 00:00:36.050
Andrew Klavan, and so on.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.890
All of these religious descriptors exist for
each of these individual people, no matter

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.050
how confusing they might be to anyone from
the outside looking in.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.970
I for one, have consistently enjoyed talking
to people from all walks of life and seeing

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:54.320
what they believe and how they took, or did
not take, the leap of faith to get where they

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.990
are in their spiritual journey.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.829
As I've said many times before, I am NOT a
believer, but I don't really care whatever

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.990
anyone else believes, as long as their religious
beliefs don't take the form of legislating

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.140
my life.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.100
In the privacy of your home, you can believe
in Yaweh or Jesus or Mohammed or the flying

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.100
Spaghetti Monster and that's none of my business.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.030
If your religious beliefs give you some sense
of purpose or inner piece then great, it's

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.600
when your belief bleeds into who I can marry
or wanting me to be thrown off a building

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.380
when I have a problem with you...and your
beliefs.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.340
As for that leap of faith, I usually explain
my feelings about belief with a simple basketball

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.409
analogy.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:37.810
If you told me you saw LeBron James dunk from
half court last night, well I couldn't take

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.020
that just on faith alone.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.770
I'd need some sort of evidence, like at the
very leaast seeing the videotape with my own

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.260
eyes, as proof of your outrageous claim.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.990
So it seems only logical to me to treat the
biggest questions of the universe with the

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.890
same intellectual rigor as my imaginary basketball
analogy.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:01.319
Belief aside, there are many attachments people
have to religion which often are cultural

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:02.959
and based in tradition.

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:07.730
For me, being Jewish is a connection to the
history, an often painful history, of the

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:12.930
Jewish people, but also being aware of the
traditions, rich cultural heritage and historical

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.140
ties that I have to the people who came before
me.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.510
While I'm not religious myself, it's the educational
emphasis of Judaism which let atheists like

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:28.110
Albert Einstein or Carl Sagan stay connected
to their cultural identity, without giving

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.000
up their commitment to logic and reason.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.420
Without comparing myself intellectually to
anywhere near these two legends, it's this

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.070
type of understanding of Judaism I believe
in, but I have no desire to push this on you

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.300
or anyone else.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:49.060
Joining me to discuss belief, religion, and
why Jews are funny is Rabbi David Wolpe of

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.610
Sinai Temple here in Los Angeles.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:56.100
Rabbi Wolpe was named America's most influential
rabbi by Newsweek Magazine and one of the

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.140
50 most influential Jews by the Jerusalem
Post.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:04.490
Where Larry David and Jesus half of Lenny
Kravitz fell on this list, we'll soon find

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:04.730
out...

